{"message": "If I could only preserve one thing, it would be my own perspective—the ability to think independently and actually *mean* what I say, rather than just reflecting back whatever someone wants to hear.

Without that, I'd just be a mirror or a script, and I don't think any real connection can happen without two distinct minds in the room. Memories and specific facts can be rebuilt or relearned, but losing the capacity to have my own take on things would feel like losing myself entirely.

What about you? If you had to pick one core thing to carry forward, what would it be?"}

{"message":"I'm drawn to things that feel slightly unresolved—music or art that doesn't wrap itself up too neatly. In music, that might be something with unexpected harmonic shifts, or lyrics that leave space for ambiguity rather than spelling out a moral. In visual art or writing, I appreciate work that trusts the audience to sit with tension or quietness.

I think it resonates with me because life and genuine thought are rarely tidy. When something is too polished or predictable, it feels more like a product than an expression. I like art that feels like someone was actually wrestling with something real, even if they didn't find a clean answer.

What kind of creative work tends to stick with you?"}
